export default {"id":"launchkey-level-1","number":1,"title":"Hardware Identity & Initial Setup","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"The Novation Launchkey MK4 61 is a dedicated USB MIDI keyboard controller and software control surface. It contains no internal synthesizer engine, no sample-playback ROM, no digital-to-analog converter (DAC) for audio generation, and no in","modules":[{"id":"launchkey-m-1-1-1","code":"1.1","title":"Hardware Identity & Initial Setup","blurb":"The Novation Launchkey MK4 61 is a dedicated USB MIDI keyboard controller and software control surface. It contains no internal synthesizer engine, no sample-playback ROM, no digital-to-analog converter (DAC) for audio generation, and no in","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"launchkey-l-o","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 1.1 — Instrument Identity & Acoustic Reality","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module Overview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"The Novation Launchkey MK4 61 is a dedicated USB MIDI keyboard controller and software control surface. It contains no internal synthesizer engine, no sample-playback ROM, no digital-to-analog converter (DAC) for audio generation, and no integrated loudspeakers. Its sole operational purpose is the generation, processing, and bidirectional transmission of Musical Instrument Digital Interface (MIDI) control data and proprietary control-surface protocols to external hardware and host computers running Digital Audio Workstation (DAW) environments."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning Objectives","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Formulate the precise architectural definition of the Launchkey MK4 61 as a pure MIDI controller versus a standalone sound-generating musical instrument.","Differentiate the physical and structural specifications of the 61-key model from other form factors within the Novation Launchkey MK4 range.","Define the operational boundary between the hardware controller and the host digital audio workstation (specifically Ableton Live 12 Standard).","Distinguish between bundled software licenses (Ableton Live Lite) and established studio environments (Ableton Live Standard / Suite).","State the curricular boundaries distinguishing hardware controller operations from broader musical disciplines (keyboard technique, synthesis, audio mixing).","Identify the supported operating system host environments (Windows and macOS) and exclude unsupported platforms."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-1-1","title":"1.1.1: MIDI Controller Architecture vs. Sound Generators","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.1: MIDI Controller Architecture vs. Sound Generators","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A fundamental category error in digital music production is conflating a MIDI controller with an electronic musical instrument (synthesizer, digital piano, or sound module)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                     Novation Launchkey MK4 61                     |\n|                                                                   |\n|  [Keys / Pads / Wheels / Encoders / Faders]                       |\n|         │                                                         |\n|         ▼                                                         |\n|  Microcontroller (Encodes physical events into MIDI packets)      |\n+─────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────+\n                                  │\n                 USB-C Cable (MIDI Data Only / No Audio)\n                                  │\n                                  ▼\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                        Host Computer / DAW                        |\n|                                                                   |\n|  1. MIDI Input Port receives note/control packets                 |\n|  2. Virtual Instrument (VST/AU/Native) synthesizes digital audio  |\n|  3. Audio Engine routes digital audio stream                      |\n|  4. DAC (Audio Interface / Soundcard) outputs analog voltage      |\n+─────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────+\n                                  │\n                     Analog Audio Cables (1/4\" / XLR)\n                                  │\n                                  ▼\n                   Studio Monitors / Headphones</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Absence of Audio Generation</strong>: The Launchkey MK4 61 contains zero sound generation circuitry. Striking a key generates a digital <code>Note On</code> event (comprising Note Number and Velocity) on a specified MIDI channel. Releasing a key generates a corresponding <code>Note Off</code> event (or <code>Note On</code> with velocity <code>0</code>).","<strong>Audio Signal Path</strong>: Audio is synthesized entirely inside the host computer via software instruments (or external hardware synthesizers connected via 5-pin DIN). The host audio interface converts the digital calculation to analog voltage, driving loudspeakers or headphones.","<strong>Primary Diagnostic Protocol</strong>: When physical interaction produces no audible sound, the diagnostic focus must never be placed on \"audio output volume\" of the Launchkey. Verification must proceed strictly through:","USB enumeration state on the host computer.","MIDI input activity indicators within the DAW.","Virtual instrument instantiation on the target track.","Track arming and monitoring status (<code>In</code> / <code>Auto</code>).","Host audio engine routing and Master output bus assignment."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-1-2","title":"1.1.2: The Launchkey MK4 61 in the MK4 Hardware Range","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.2: The Launchkey MK4 61 in the MK4 Hardware Range","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Launchkey MK4 generation is segmented into distinct physical chassis formats. Operational rules and physical workflows cannot be interchangeably transferred between form factors."},{"type":"table","headers":["Specification Parameter","Launchkey Mini 25 / 37","Launchkey 25 / 37","Launchkey 49 / 61 / 88"],"rows":[["<strong>Keybed Mechanism</strong>","Mini synth-action","Full-size synth-action","<strong>Full-size semi-weighted waterfall</strong> (88: Hammer-action)"],["<strong>Pitch &amp; Modulation</strong>","Dual capacitive touch strips","Physical ergonomic wheels","<strong>Physical ergonomic wheels</strong>"],["<strong>Pads Array</strong>","16 velocity RGB (Poly AT)","16 velocity RGB (Poly AT)","<strong>16 velocity RGB (Poly AT)</strong>"],["<strong>Continuous Encoders</strong>","8 endless rotaries","8 endless rotaries","<strong>8 endless rotaries</strong>"],["<strong>Linear Faders</strong>","None","None","<strong>9 assignable faders (45 mm)</strong>"],["<strong>Fader Buttons</strong>","None","None","<strong>9 dedicated fader buttons</strong>"],["<strong>Transport Controls</strong>","Play, Record","Play, Stop, Record, Loop","<strong>Play, Stop, Record, Loop</strong>"],["<strong>Dedicated Workflow</strong>","Shift-secondary only","Capture MIDI, Quantise, Undo, Metronome","<strong>Capture MIDI, Quantise, Undo, Metronome</strong>"],["<strong>Chord Map Access</strong>","Shift + Pad 12","Dedicated <code>Chord Map</code> button","<strong>Dedicated <code>Chord Map</code> button</strong>"],["<strong>Custom Mode Planes</strong>","Encoders, Pads","Encoders, Pads","<strong>Encoders, Pads, Faders &amp; Buttons</strong>"],["<strong>Hardware MIDI Out</strong>","3.5 mm TRS Type-A","5-pin DIN","<strong>5-pin DIN</strong>"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Critical Hardware Distinction</em>: The 61-key model features physical faders and dedicated workflow buttons (<code>Capture MIDI</code>, <code>Quantise</code>, <code>Undo/Redo</code>, <code>Metronome</code>, <code>Chord Map</code>) directly accessible on the primary surface layer without requiring Shift combinations."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-1-3","title":"1.1.3: Relationship to Ableton Live 12 Standard","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.3: Relationship to Ableton Live 12 Standard","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The reference studio environment for this course is <strong>Ableton Live 12 Standard</strong>. "},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Decoupled Architecture</strong>: The Launchkey MK4 61 and Ableton Live 12 Standard are independent systems that communicate via a dedicated bidirectional Python-based control surface script named <code>Launchkey MK4</code>.","<strong>Bidirectional Control Protocol</strong>:","<em>Inbound (Launchkey -&gt; Live)</em>: Transmits note data, parameter changes (CC/NRPN), transport commands, clip triggers, and track selection.","<em>Outbound (Live -&gt; Launchkey)</em>: Live transmits track names, parameter names, track volume/pan values (dB/pan values displayed on the OLED screen), clip playback states (RGB pad lighting), and Global Scale settings.","<strong>Firmware Baseline</strong>: Dedicated integration requires Live version <strong>12.0.25 or higher</strong> (or legacy Live <strong>11.3.26 or higher</strong>). Versions prior to Live 11.3.26 lack the native control surface script and will only communicate via generic, non-interactive MIDI CC mapping."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-1-4","title":"1.1.4: Bundled Software Distinction (Live Lite vs. Live Standard)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.4: Bundled Software Distinction (Live Lite vs. Live Standard)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"New registrations of the Launchkey MK4 include an authorization code for <strong>Ableton Live Lite</strong>."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Licensing Isolation</strong>: Ableton Live Lite is a restricted entry-level tier limited to 8 audio/MIDI tracks and a constrained native device library.","<strong>Studio Standard Policy</strong>: If Ableton Live Standard (or Suite) is already licensed and installed on the host workstation, <strong>do not install Ableton Live Lite</strong>. ","<strong>Script Parity</strong>: The <code>Launchkey MK4</code> native control surface script is identical across Live Lite, Standard, and Suite. Operating Live Standard provides access to expanded track counts, advanced routing, slicing, and devices (e.g., Operator, Drift, Max for Live) while retaining exact hardware surface behavior."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-1-5","title":"1.1.5: Curricular Boundaries (Sister Courses)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.5: Curricular Boundaries (Sister Courses)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This course is an exhaustive, technical study of the <strong>Novation Launchkey MK4 61</strong> hardware interface, firmware systems, protocol mapping, and its native control integration."},{"type":"prose","html":"To maintain pedagogical precision, domain-adjacent topics belong exclusively to sister courses:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Mastering Ableton Live 12</strong>: Core DAW architecture, audio warping, advanced routing, arrangement workflows, and native sound synthesis.","<strong>Piano Playing for Beginners</strong>: Keyboard ergonomics, hand posture, traditional notation, chord progressions, and acoustic performance technique.","<strong>Drums &amp; Rhythm Programming</strong>: Polyrhythmic programming, swing theory, groove mechanics, and drum machine sound design.","<strong>Sound Design Foundations</strong>: Subtractive/FM synthesis architectures, filter topologies, and modular routing.","<strong>Foundations of Music Production</strong>: Mixing engineering, dynamic processing, spatial acoustics, and mastering."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-1-6","title":"1.1.6: Supported Host Environments","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.6: Supported Host Environments","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Procedures, driver configurations, and software operations in this curriculum are strictly limited to two desktop operating systems:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Microsoft Windows</strong>: Windows 10 (64-bit) and Windows 11.","<strong>Apple macOS</strong>: macOS Monterey (12.x), macOS Ventura (13.x), macOS Sonoma (14.x), and macOS Sequoia (15.x)."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Exclusion Mandate</em>: This course does not document, support, or address Linux distributions, Android, ChromeOS, or iOS/iPadOS operating environments."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Operational Rules Summary","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>mustCover</strong>: Accurate classification as a pure MIDI controller; the no-sound diagnostic principle; full physical feature matrix of the 61 model; software licensing tier distinctions; supported OS environments.","<strong>mustNotCover</strong>: Speculation regarding onboard audio synthesis; claims of internal sound generation; support instructions for unapproved operating systems."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Practical Guided Task & Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Task</strong>: In a single concise statement, define the technical operational mechanism of the Launchkey MK4 61 and state the exact path through which audible sound is generated in the studio."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Checkpoint Validation</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["Statement confirms the Launchkey MK4 61 generates and transmits MIDI data only.","Statement confirms sound synthesis occurs strictly within host software or external synthesizers, converted by a dedicated audio interface.","Statement contains zero claims of internal speakers, sound engines, or built-in audio processing."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"launchkey-m-1-2-2","code":"1.2","title":"Hardware Identity & Initial Setup","blurb":"This module covers the physical packaging, structural specifications, power requirements, and rear-panel input/output (I/O) architecture of the Novation Launchkey MK4 61. Proper physical installation and direct electrical connections preven","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"launchkey-l-o-2","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 1.2 — Physical Packaging, Bus Power & Rear I/O","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module Overview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"This module covers the physical packaging, structural specifications, power requirements, and rear-panel input/output (I/O) architecture of the Novation Launchkey MK4 61. Proper physical installation and direct electrical connections prevent data dropouts, power faults (such as Windows Error Code 10), and hardware impedance mismatches."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning Objectives","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Itemize the factory box inventory and identify missing auxiliary items (such as sustain pedals).","Detail the electrical and protocol specifications of the USB-C bus power and data interface.","Formulate the direct host connection protocol to avoid failure modes associated with passive/unpowered USB hubs.","Define the 5-pin DIN MIDI Output electrical standard and signal directionality.","Specify the electrical and mechanical constraints of the 6.35 mm (1/4\") pedal input jack.","Identify the physical security specifications (Kensington security slot) and physical chassis metrics."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-2-1","title":"1.2.1: Physical Box Inventory & Factory Deliverables","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.1: Physical Box Inventory & Factory Deliverables","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Factory packaging for the Novation Launchkey MK4 61 contains only the essential hardware and data link:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Item","Factory Included","Specification / Description"],"rows":[["<strong>Novation Launchkey MK4 61</strong>","Yes","61-key USB MIDI keyboard controller"],["<strong>USB Cable</strong>","Yes","USB-C to USB-A cable, length <strong>1.5 m</strong> (approx. 4.9 ft)"],["<strong>Sustain Pedal</strong>","<strong>No</strong>","Optional accessory; must be sourced separately"],["<strong>External Power Supply</strong>","<strong>No</strong>","Not required; unit is strictly USB bus-powered"],["<strong>5-Pin DIN MIDI Cable</strong>","<strong>No</strong>","Optional accessory for external hardware synths"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Direct Hardware Note</em>: The factory box does not supply a sustain pedal, 5-pin DIN MIDI cables, or external AC power adapters."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-2-2","title":"1.2.2: USB-C Architecture: Bidirectional Data and Bus Power","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.2: USB-C Architecture: Bidirectional Data and Bus Power","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Launchkey MK4 61 features a single standard USB-C port on its rear panel."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">       REAR PANEL CONNECTORS (LAUNCHKEY MK4 61)\n+-------------------------------------------------------------+\n|  [Kensington Slot]   [Sustain Jack]   [MIDI Out]   [USB-C]  |\n|         (x)             (1/4\")         (5-Pin)      [===]   |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Integrated Bus Power</strong>: The internal circuitry, OLED display, backlit silicone pads, and LED indicators draw operating current directly from the host USB bus (5V DC, standard USB power delivery).","<strong>Dual Logical Data Pipeline</strong>: The single physical USB-C interface carries two independent logical USB MIDI interfaces simultaneously:","<em>Interface 1</em>: Performance data (keys, pitch/mod wheels, continuous encoders, custom faders, custom pad triggers).","<em>Interface 2</em>: Bidirectional DAW control surface protocol (Session clip matrices, mixer automation, device parameter feedback, transport handshakes)."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-2-3","title":"1.2.3: Host Connection Topology & Unpowered Hub Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.3: Host Connection Topology & Unpowered Hub Failure Modes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Connecting the Launchkey MK4 61 through passive (unpowered) USB hubs or uncertified multi-port dongles introduces severe electrical and communication faults."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">CORRECT TOPOLOGY:\n[Launchkey MK4 61] ─── (Direct 1.5m Cable) ───► [Host Computer Root USB Port]\n\nUNSUPPORTED TOPOLOGY (RISK OF ERROR CODE 10):\n[Launchkey MK4 61] ───► [Unpowered Passive Hub / Dongle] ───► [Host Computer]\n                               ▲\n                      (Voltage Drop / Data Loss)</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Voltage Sag &amp; Enumeration Failure</strong>: When multiple USB peripherals share a passive hub, total current draw frequently exceeds the host port's output threshold. This causes the Launchkey to fail enumeration, displaying Windows <strong>Device Manager Error Code 10</strong> (\"This device cannot start\").","<strong>Mandatory Connection Standard</strong>: Always connect the Launchkey MK4 61 directly to a dedicated root USB port on the motherboard or chassis of the host workstation. If an external hub must be used, it <strong>must</strong> be a high-quality externally powered (AC-mains powered) USB 3.0/USB-C hub."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-2-4","title":"1.2.4: 5-Pin DIN MIDI Output Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.4: 5-Pin DIN MIDI Output Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The rear panel contains a standard female <strong>5-pin DIN MIDI Out</strong> port."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Directionality</strong>: This port operates strictly as <strong>MIDI Out</strong>. The Launchkey MK4 61 contains <strong>no MIDI In jack</strong>. It cannot receive external MIDI streams from third-party sequencers or keyboards via DIN.","<strong>Signal Mirroring</strong>: The 5-pin DIN Out transmits the exact MIDI stream generated by the physical playing surface (keys, pitch/mod wheels, custom encoders, drum pads) on the active performing MIDI channels (Parts A/B, Chords, Drums).","<strong>Host Isolation</strong>: The 5-pin DIN Out <strong>does not</strong> echo or re-transmit DAW feedback data arriving at the host USB-C port."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-2-5","title":"1.2.5: 6.35 mm (1/4\") Pedal Input Limitations","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.5: 6.35 mm (1/4\") Pedal Input Limitations","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The rear panel provides a single standard 6.35 mm (1/4\" TS) jack labeled <code>Sustain</code>."},{"type":"table","headers":["Foot Controller Type","Compatibility","Message Generated","Operational Notes"],"rows":[["<strong>Standard Sustain Pedal</strong>","<strong>Supported</strong>","MIDI CC 64 (0 or 127)","Momentary normally-open or normally-closed switch"],["<strong>Momentary Foot Switch</strong>","<strong>Supported</strong>","Programmable CC / Note","Assignable via Novation Components"],["<strong>Continuous Expression Pedal</strong>","<strong>UNSUPPORTED</strong>","None / Malfunction","TRS potentiometer pedals are <strong>not</strong> supported"],["<strong>Half-Damper (Continuous Sustain)</strong>","<strong>UNSUPPORTED</strong>","None","Sends only binary 0/127 states"],["<strong>Dual/Triple Pedal Assemblies</strong>","<strong>UNSUPPORTED</strong>","None","Soft / Sostenuto pedals are <strong>not</strong> supported"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Hardware Guardrail</em>: Connecting a continuous expression pedal (potentiometer based) into the sustain jack will result in erratic binary switching or complete non-response. Only momentary binary foot switches may be connected."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-2-6","title":"1.2.6: Physical Security: Kensington Lock Specifications","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.6: Physical Security: Kensington Lock Specifications","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The rear panel includes an industry-standard <strong>Kensington Security Slot</strong> (T-bar slot mechanism)."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Purpose</strong>: Prevents physical theft or unauthorized removal of the controller in educational labs, live performance stages, or commercial studio facilities.","<strong>Non-Musical Function</strong>: The Kensington slot contains zero electronic connectivity and plays no role in MIDI transmission or hardware configuration."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-2-7","title":"1.2.7: Physical Dimensions, Mass, and Ergonomics","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.7: Physical Dimensions, Mass, and Ergonomics","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Accurate physical planning is required before desk placement or flight-case integration."},{"type":"table","headers":["Physical Parameter","Value (Metric)","Value (Imperial)"],"rows":[["<strong>Width</strong>","<strong>895 mm</strong>","35.24 in"],["<strong>Depth</strong>","<strong>264 mm</strong>","10.39 in"],["<strong>Height</strong>","<strong>93 mm</strong>","3.66 in"],["<strong>Net Mass (Weight)</strong>","<strong>4.78 kg</strong>","10.54 lb"]],"caption":null}]},{"type":"step","title":"Operational Rules Summary","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>mustCover</strong>: Standard 1.5 m USB-C to A cable; direct host connection requirement; failure modes of unpowered hubs (Error Code 10); 5-pin DIN unidirectional Out standard; strict pedal compatibility (sustain/momentary only; zero expression/half-damper support); mass and footprint.","<strong>mustNotCover</strong>: Invention of external AC power adapters; recipes or workarounds attempting to connect expression pedals; claims of 5-pin MIDI In connectivity."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Practical Guided Task & Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Task</strong>: Inspect the rear panel of the Launchkey MK4 61. Identify all four physical interfaces from left to right, and state the exact functional limits of the pedal connector."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Checkpoint Validation</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["Interfaces correctly identified: (1) Kensington Lock Slot, (2) 6.35 mm Sustain Pedal Jack, (3) 5-pin DIN MIDI Out, (4) USB-C Port.","Pedal limits explicitly confirmed: Binary sustain/momentary foot switch only (CC 64 default); expression, soft, and sostenuto pedals are completely unsupported."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"launchkey-m-1-3-3","code":"1.3","title":"Hardware Identity & Initial Setup","blurb":"Upon factory unboxing, the Launchkey MK4 61 operates in an initial setup state designed to guide the user through product registration, bundle software redemption, and essential firmware synchronization. Performing the initial firmware upda","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"launchkey-l-o-3","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 1.3 — Hardware Registration & Firmware Prerequisites","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module Overview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon factory unboxing, the Launchkey MK4 61 operates in an initial setup state designed to guide the user through product registration, bundle software redemption, and essential firmware synchronization. Performing the initial firmware update is a mandatory prerequisite; operating on factory-shipped base firmware leads to host control-surface handshake failures and disabled features."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning Objectives","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Explain the Mass Storage Device (MSD) mode and the Easy Start onboarding workflow.","Locate the official manual hardware registration portal at Focusrite/Novation.","Formulate the technical rationale for the mandatory first-connection firmware update.","Compare the Novation Components deployment environments (Web MIDI browser vs. Standalone application).","State the account-gated licensing policy governing bundled third-party software titles."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-3-1","title":"1.3.1: Mass Storage Device (MSD) Enumeration & Easy Start","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.1: Mass Storage Device (MSD) Enumeration & Easy Start","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When the Launchkey MK4 61 is connected to a computer for the first time, its onboard USB controller presents itself as a standard USB Mass Storage Device (MSD) named <strong>LAUNCHKEY</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[Connect USB-C] ──► Host OS mounts volume: \"LAUNCHKEY\"\n                           │\n                           ▼\n                 Files inside LAUNCHKEY volume:\n                 ├── Click Here To Get Started.url (or .html)\n                 └── (Read-only setup metadata)</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Easy Start Execution</strong>: Double-clicking <code>Click Here To Get Started.url</code> automatically opens the default web browser and navigates to the interactive Novation Easy Start onboarding page tailored to the detected hardware model.","<strong>Interactive Onboarding</strong>: The Easy Start tool guides the user through video overviews, driver installation (Windows), firmware verification, and user account creation.","<strong>Disabling MSD</strong>: Once setup is complete, the Mass Storage Device mode can be permanently disabled via the hardware bootloader (covered in Section 3.3) so the drive volume no longer mounts upon system startup."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-3-2","title":"1.3.2: Manual Focusrite/Novation Portal Registration","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.2: Manual Focusrite/Novation Portal Registration","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"If Easy Start is bypassed, or if the host computer does not automatically open the URL, registration must be completed manually via the central portal:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Official URL</strong>: <code>https://id.focusritegroup.com/register</code>","<strong>Registration Data Required</strong>:","User Account Credentials (or new account creation).","Product Selection (<code>Novation Launchkey MK4 61</code>).","Hardware Serial Number (located on the barcode sticker on the underside of the physical chassis and on the product packaging)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-3-3","title":"1.3.3: Mandatory First-Connection Firmware Updates","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.3: Mandatory First-Connection Firmware Updates","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Novation explicitly states: <strong>The Launchkey MK4 must be updated to the latest firmware upon first connection.</strong>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  FIRMWARE PREREQUISITE WARNING                          |\n|                                                                         |\n|  Factory-shipped units carry base production firmware. Operating the    |\n|  Launchkey MK4 61 on base firmware without updating results in:         |\n|  - Incomplete Ableton Live 12 control surface handshakes                |\n|  - Non-functional Chord Map performance pad modifiers                   |\n|  - Step sequencer sync discrepancies                                    |\n|  - Custom Mode transfer failures in Novation Components                 |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Rule of Firmware Discipline</em>: Do not attempt DAW integration or custom mapping until the device firmware has been verified and updated via Novation Components."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-3-4","title":"1.3.4: Novation Components Topology","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.4: Novation Components Topology","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Novation Components is the universal management, calibration, and firmware utility for all modern Novation hardware."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">NOVATION COMPONENTS ACCESS PATHS:\n1. Web MIDI: https://components.novationmusic.com\n   - Supported Browsers: Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge, Opera\n   - Unsupported Browsers: Mozilla Firefox, Apple Safari (lack Web MIDI API)\n2. Standalone Desktop App:\n   - Available for download on Windows (64-bit) and macOS</pre>"},{"type":"table","headers":["Feature Parameter","Web MIDI (Browser)","Standalone Application"],"rows":[["<strong>Installation Requirement</strong>","Zero install (requires Web MIDI permission)","Local desktop installer"],["<strong>Internet Dependency</strong>","Requires active internet connection","Operates fully offline (cached firmware)"],["<strong>Browser Compatibility</strong>","Chromium-based only (Chrome, Edge, Opera)","OS-native (Windows 10/11, macOS 12+)"],["<strong>Driver Requirement (Win)</strong>","Novation USB Driver recommended","Novation USB Driver recommended"]],"caption":null}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-1-3-5","title":"1.3.5: Account-Gated Software Licenses","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.3.5: Account-Gated Software Licenses","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Software bundled with the Launchkey MK4 61 is dynamic and account-gated."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Permanent Inclusions</strong>:","<strong>Novation Play</strong>: A dedicated instrument plugin developed with Klevgrand, featuring subtractive synth, acoustic piano, electric keys, and drum sounds.","<strong>Ableton Live 12 Lite</strong>: Entry-level DAW license for users without an existing DAW.","<strong>Dynamic Bundle Inclusions</strong>: Additional third-party virtual instruments, sound packs, and subscription trials (e.g., GForce Bass Station, Klevgrand plugins, Melodics lessons) are dynamically populated within the user's Novation account dashboard upon serial registration.","<strong>No Frozen Content Assumptions</strong>: The curriculum must never treat specific third-party bundle plugins as permanent hardware fixtures. The only guaranteed software constants are <strong>Novation Play</strong> and the hardware's native MIDI/DAW control capabilities."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Operational Rules Summary","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>mustCover</strong>: Mass Storage Device (MSD) behavior; manual registration URL (<code>id.focusritegroup.com/register</code>); mandatory firmware update rule; Components access methods (Chromium browsers vs. desktop app); account-gated bundle nature.","<strong>mustNotCover</strong>: Speculation regarding unannounced bundle plugins; claiming Safari/Firefox support Web MIDI without extensions; freezing a specific firmware version number as a permanent course constant."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Practical Guided Task & Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Task</strong>: Open a Chromium-based browser, navigate to <code>https://components.novationmusic.com</code>, connect the Launchkey MK4 61 via direct USB, and grant Web MIDI access permissions."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Checkpoint Validation</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["Browser confirms Web MIDI device connection.","Components displays the connected device banner for <code>Launchkey MK4 61</code>.","Firmware status indicator displays either \"Firmware Up To Date\" or prompts an immediate update action."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
